Z||000000||william_thorniii||11-13-2000||05:39 PM||||Friends,<P>The problem is as explained above.  The engine diagnostics works properly.  Sensor readings have been looked at with my OTC monitor... so the code is truly false.  <P>I will be the first to admit that I have not done much homework on this subject so forgive my ignorance.  I have searched the archives and looked in helm/haynes/OSG manuals but have not found anything that looks to be a typical problem.  <P>I do have a multimeter, but am an electrical eg-no-ra-mous (to quote Cracker Barrel game).  I am adamently opposed to destructive testing, so please keep that in mind when giving directions.  I would prefer to live with the problem than "clip this wire and splice it together with a splice, etc."  This cars wiring appears to be factory original both in the engine compartment and under the dash (which was out recently).<P>Does anyone have any places to start?  The car runs great.  It didn't have a bulb in the gauge cluster at point of purchase.  During a recent interior restoration I replaced the bulb.  <P>I can't figure this one out.  Any suggestions?  <P>Thanks,<P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||5
Z||000001||fldevel||11-13-2000||06:34 PM||||i just got through fixing the smae problem .. the check enginelight would come on but no codes ...engine seemed to run fine .. untill one day the light came on and i steped on the gas and the car died .. i went through all the electronics.. map senser, IAC modual ..everything .. my car was down for a week i keept telling myself that it couldent be the throtle positioning senser untill i sed the hell with it and changed it ... the car is now running fine ||||reg||1
Z||000002||robertjay||11-13-2000||07:05 PM||||Here is a copy of a page in my 85 factory service manual.  Notice the Brown/White wire coming from the ECM to the check engine light.  This wire has to be grounded for the light to come on.  I would check the brown/white wire at the ECM and see if it is grounded, (It should be if the light is on) then I would unplug the wire from the harness, maybe has to have a little tang bent on the connector and then removed.  If the light still stays on, you know it is a problem with the wiring from the ECM to the light.  If the light goes out, it is most likely a problem with the ECM, in which case I can't help you  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">, sorry.<P>Hope this helps<BR>(sorry the image is so large and sort of crooked)<P>Robert<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Instrument_1.jpg"> <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||11-13-2000||07:33 PM||||Disconnect the bat, unplug the ECM, both connectors, then reconnect the bat.<P>see if it goes out now. If it doesn't then it's a wire sorted or maybe the ignition switch. (I'm not a hundred percent sure if this is tied to the ignition switch lamp test function or not.)<P>If it does go out with the ECM unplugged the ECM's got a problem.||||reg||1
Z||000004||william_thorniii||11-14-2000||08:37 AM||||Friends,<P>Thanks!  Each of those situations seem simple enough.  If worse comes to worse, I have an extra ECM (for right now anyway) in my 86 SE.  Thanks for all your input.  <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||14
